"Mar 4, 2026\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":\"·\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[10,\" min read\"]}]]}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-sm text-muted-foreground line-clamp-2\",\"children\":\"A technical walkthrough of the vLLM Triton attention backend, covering performance-portable paged attention kernels, backend selection, autotuning, CUDA graph behavior, benchmarks, and NVIDIA, AMD, and Intel support.\"}]]}]]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"28:[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"sr-only\",\"children\":\"Slack\"}]\n29:[\"$\",\"$L5\",\"Discuss\",{\"href\":\"https://discuss.vllm.ai/\",\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"className\":\"text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"ref\":\"$undefined\",\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":24,\"height\":24,\"viewBox\":\"0 0 24 24\",\"fill\":\"none\",\"stroke\":\"currentColor\",\"strokeWidth\":2,\"strokeLinecap\":\"round\",\"strokeLinejoin\":\"round\",\"className\":\"lucide lucide-message-square h-5 w-5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"path\",\"1lielz\",{\"d\":\"M21 15a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H7l-4 4V5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h14a2 2 0 0 1 2 2z\"}],\"$undefined\"]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"sr-only\",\"children\":\"Discuss\"}]]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"2a:I[8390,[\"4256\",\"static/chunks/4256-4f71aba0ec21946e.js?dpl=dpl_7QdgQp9DFQRJ35B4x4i8T56d1WeF\",\"1833\",\"static/chunks/1833-4147add9c823bcc5.js?dpl=dpl_7QdgQp9DFQRJ35B4x4i8T56d1WeF\",\"4901\",\"static/chunks/4901-3b763f5780212bc8.js?dpl=dpl_7QdgQp9DFQRJ35B4x4i8T56d1WeF\",\"5953\",\"static/chunks/app/blog/%5Bslug%5D/page-f151d94009e1749f.js?dpl=dpl_7QdgQp9DFQRJ35B4x4i8T56d1WeF\"],\"ImageZoom\"]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"26:[[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"1-introduction\",\"className\":\"text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3 scroll-mt-24\",\"children\":\"1. Introduction\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"my-4 leading-relaxed\",\"children\":\"Long-context inference is becoming essential for agentic AI, where assistants may need to reason over large code repositories and long chat histories. Agent-trace benchmarks now run from 64K all the way to 1M tokens and their KV caches are correspondingly large. Under a baseline tensor-parallel (TP) setup, this KV cache is partitioned by attention head, which puts a hard floor on how much it can shrink.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"my-4 leading-relaxed\",\"children\":[\"Modern models use one of two attention schemes, and both hit this floor. Grouped-query attention (GQA) models store a small number of KV heads, and TP can only split the KV cache down to one head per GPU; once TP exceeds the number of KV heads, the cache starts duplicating across GPUs. Multi-head latent attention (MLA) models make this even worse: MLA compresses the Key/Value into a single low-rank \",[\"$\",\"em\",null,{\"children\":\"latent\"}],\" vector shared across all query heads, so it effectively has only one KV head. Under normal TP there is nothing to split by head, meaning the latent KV cache is replicated in full across \",[\"$\",\"em\",null,{\"children\":\"every\"}],\" TP rank. In both cases the duplicated KV cache eats into GPU memory, leaving very little room to serve additional requests. This caps the number of concurrent requests the system can handle, driving down throughput and pushing up cost per token.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"my-4 leading-relaxed\",\"children\":\"Decode Context Parallelism addresses this by splitting KV cache across the GPUs so each GPU stores and reads only part of the KV cache. This frees up GPU memory, allowing each GPU to take on more requests and thus run at a larger batch size. On systems with high-bandwidth GPU-to-GPU interconnects, this helps preserve interactive responsiveness while serving many long-context agents at once.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"my-4 leading-relaxed\",\"children\":\"vLLM has supported DCP for almost a year, but we are writing this blog now to highlight the feature, along with the recent improvements and advancements we have made to it, because the rise of long-context agentic use cases has made its benefits more relevant than ever.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"figure\",null,{\"className\":\"my-6\",\"style\":\"$undefined\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L2a\",null,{\"src\":\"/blog-assets/figures/2026-07-27-decode-context-parallelism/kv-parallelism-overview.svg\",\"alt\":\"\",\"className\":\"rounded-lg max-w-full\",\"loading\":\"lazy\"}],\"\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"2-performance-results\",\"className\":\"text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3 scroll-mt-24\",\"children\":\"2. Performance Results\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"my-4 leading-relaxed\",\"children\":\"To quantify the benefit of Decode Context Parallelism, we compared a baseline tensor-parallel deployment against DCP on an identical set of GPUs, holding the model, hardware, and workload fixed and varying only how the KV cache is sharded during decode.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"figure\",null,{\"className\":\"my-6\",\"style\":\"$undefined\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L2a\",null,{\"src\":\"/blog-assets/figures/2026-07-27-decode-context-parallelism/figure-1.png\",\"alt\":\"\",\"className\":\"rounded-lg max-w-full\",\"loading\":\"lazy\"}],\"\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"figure\",null,{\"className\":\"my-6\",\"style\":\"$undefined\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L2a\",null,{\"src\":\"/blog-assets/figures/2026-07-27-decode-context-parallelism/figure-2.png\",\"alt\":\"\",\"className\":\"rounded-lg max-w-full\",\"loading\":\"lazy\"}],\"\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"21-dataset\",\"className\":\"text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2 scroll-mt-24\",\"children\":\"2.1 Dataset\"}],\"\\n\",\"$L2b\",\"\\n\",\"$L2c\",\"\\n\",\"$L2d\",\"\\n\",\"$L2e\",\"\\n\",\"$L2f\",\"\\n\",\"$L30\",\"\\n\",\"$L31\",\"\\n\",\"$L32\",\"\\n\",\"$L33\",\"\\n\",\"$L34\",\"\\n\",\"$L35\",\"\\n\",\"$L36\",\"\\n\",\"$L37\",\"\\n\",\"$L38\",\"\\n\",\"$L39\",\"\\n\",\"$L3a\",\"\\n\",\"$L3b\",\"\\n\",\"$L3c\",\"\\n\",\"$L3d\",\"\\n\",\"$L3e\",\"\\n\",\"$L3f\",\"\\n\",\"$L40\",\"\\n\",\"$L41\",\"\\n\",\"$L42\",\"\\n\",\"$L43\",\"\\n\",\"$L44\",\"\\n\",\"$L45\",\"\\n\",\"$L46\",\"\\n\",\"$L47\",\"\\n\",\"$L48\",\"\\n\",\"$L49\",\"\\n\",\"$L4a\",\"\\n\",\"$L4b\",\"\\n\",\"$L4c\",\"\\n\",\"$L4d\",\"\\n\",\"$L4e\",\"\\n\",\"$L4f\",\"\\n\",\"$L50\",\"\\n\",\"$L51\",\"\\n\",\"$L52\",\"\\n\",\"$L53\",\"\\n\",\"$L54\",\"\\n\",\"$L55\",\"\\n\",\"$L56\"]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"57:I[2644,[\"4256\",\"static/chunks/4256-4f71aba0ec21946e.js?dpl=dpl_7QdgQp9DFQRJ35B4x4i8T56d1WeF\",\"1833\",\"static/chunks/1833-4147add9c823bcc5.js?dpl=dpl_7QdgQp9DFQRJ35B4x4i8T56d1WeF\",\"4901\",\"static/chunks/4901-3b763f5780212bc8.js?dpl=dpl_7QdgQp9DFQRJ35B4x4i8T56d1WeF\",\"5953\",\"static/chunks/app/blog/%5Bslug%5D/page-f151d94009e1749f.js?dpl=dpl_7QdgQp9DFQRJ35B4x4i8T56d1WeF\"],\"CopyCodeButton\"]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"2b:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"my-4 leading-relaxed\",\"children\":[\"The dataset is a publicly available agentic long-context trace in Mooncake trace format, \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://github.com/ai-dynamo/dynamo/blob/main/recipes/kimi-k2.6/perf/traces/64k_400_90kv_agent_new_noschedule_short_15perc.jsonl\",\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"className\":\"text-vllm-blue hover:underline\",\"children\":\"published here\"}],\". See \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://github.com/ai-dynamo/dynamo/blob/main/recipes/kimi-k2.6/perf/README.md#dataset\",\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"className\":\"text-vllm-blue hover:underline\",\"children\":\"this section\"}],\" for more details on the dataset. It ships as JSONL where each line is a single request with \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"className\":\"px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded-md bg-muted font-mono text-sm break-all\",\"children\":\"input_length\"}],\", \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"className\":\"px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded-md bg-muted font-mono text-sm break-all\",\"children\":\"output_length\"}],\", and \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"className\":\"px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded-md bg-muted font-mono text-sm break-all\",\"children\":\"hash_ids\"}],\" fields, so it can be replayed directly with any Mooncake-compatible harness (e.g. \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"className\":\"px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded-md bg-muted font-mono text-sm break-all\",\"children\":\"aiperf --custom-dataset-type mooncake_trace\"}],\"). The \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"className\":\"px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded-md bg-muted font-mono text-sm break-all\",\"children\":\"hash_ids\"}],\" field encodes shared prefix blocks, making it well-suited for benchmarking KV-cache reuse and prefix-caching behavior.\"]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"2c:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"my-4 leading-relaxed\",\"children\":\"It's an agentic multi-turn workload of long inputs paired with short generations, chosen to reflect realistic long-horizon agent behavior. Inputs are centered around a median of ~67k tokens and paired with short ~400-token outputs, but the input distribution is bimodal rather than uniformly huge: roughly half the requests sit at 64k+ (≈53%, with a heavy tail reaching ~1M tokens) and half are short-to-mid (≈47% under 64k, ~18% under 8k). About 8% of requests exceed 128k and ~3–4% exceed 256k.\"}]\n2d:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"22-benefits-of-decode-context-parallelism\",\"className\":\"text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2 scroll-mt-24\",\"children\":\"2.2 Benefits of Decode Context Parallelism\"}]\n2e:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"my-4 leading-relaxed\",\"children\":\"We ran an experiment on a single 8×B200 node serving Kimi K2.6 in NVFP4 with vLLM, sweeping request concurrency from 16 to 512 (see table below). DCP sustains far higher concurrency and delivers markedly higher throughput per GPU across the entire throughput–interactivity Pareto frontier.\"}]\n2f:[\"$\",\"figure\",null,{\"className\":\"my-6\",\"style\":\"$undefined\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L2a\",null,{\"src\":\"/blog-assets/figures/2026-07-27-decode-context-parallelism/figure-3.png\",\"alt\":\"\",\"className\":\"rounded-lg max-w-full\",\"loading\":\"lazy\"}],\"\"]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"30:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"my-4 leading-relaxed\",\"children\":[\"The difference comes down to where the KV cache lives. Baseline TP replicates the KV cache on every GPU, so peak memory fills quickly. It reaches 100% at a concurrency of 64 and hits a wall, and throughput plateaus near 1,863 tok/s/GPU because no additional requests can fit. On the other hand, DCP shards the KV cache along the sequence dimension, so each GPU stores only 1/N of every request's KV. This allows space on the GPU to support more incoming requests. As a result, even at high concurrencies DCP keeps scaling where TP hits a wall. DCP reaches 6,091 tok/s/GPU at c512 while still sitting at just 82% KV usage. \",[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"The core value of DCP is that it sustains far higher concurrency, even on long-context runs, precisely the regime where replicated-KV TP runs out of memory first.\"}]]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"31:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"23-comparison-by-sequence-length\",\"className\":\"text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2 scroll-mt-24\",\"children\":\"2.3 Comparison by Sequence Length\"}]\n32:[\"$\",\"figure\",null,{\"className\":\"my-6\",\"style\":\"$undefined\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L2a\",null,{\"src\":\"/blog-assets/figures/2026-07-27-decode-context-parallelism/figure-4.png\",\"alt\":\"\",\"className\":\"rounded-lg max-w-full\",\"loading\":\"lazy\"}],\"\"]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"33:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"my-4 leading-relaxed\",\"children\":[\"We also plotted performance against full sequence length (input + output). The figure shows a single throughput–interactivity Pareto frontier with requests grouped into five length bands (\u003c32k, 32–64k, 64–128k, 128–200k, and 200k+) so we can see how performance shifts with context length. \",[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"DCP keeps a high, stable frontier even in the 200k+ range\"}],\", with the curves for short and long buckets nearly overlapping: throughput scales with concurrency while per-user speed stays usable at the long context lengths where the replicated-KV baseline runs out of memory and cannot scale.\"]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"34:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"3-challenges-of-serving-long-contexts\",\"className\":\"text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3 scroll-mt-24\",\"children\":\"3. Challenges of Serving Long Contexts\"}]\n35:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"my-4 leading-relaxed\",\"children\":[\"Under tensor parallelism, the KV cache is partitioned \",[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"by the attention head\"}],\". Each KV head owns its own separate K and V tensors, and the head is the smallest unit you can hand to a GPU. A standard TP has no mechanism to slice a single head's KV cache. So if you have K KV heads, you can give each GPU a distinct subset of those heads, but only down to the point where every GPU holds one head. Once TP goes beyond K, there aren't enough distinct heads to go around, so two or more GPUs end up holding a copy of the same head's KV cache instead of a unique slice.\"]}]\n36:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"4-what-is-dcp\",\"className\":\"text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3 scroll-mt-24\",\"children\":\"4. What is DCP?\"}]\n37:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"my-4 leading-relaxed\",\"children\":[\"Unlike pure TP methods, DCP is able to split KV cache across GPUs by sequence (context) dimension. Each GPU is made responsible for the KV cache of a chunk of \",[\"$\",\"em\",null,{\"children\":\"token positions\"}],\" from the same sequence. For a single 200K-token request, GPU 0 might hold the cache for tokens 0–50K, GPU 1 for tokens 50K–100K, GPU 2 for 100K–150K, and GPU 3 for 150K–200K. By sharding KV cache, the KV cache footprint per GPU keeps shrinking as you add GPUs, freeing the memory that lets you raise the batch size and serve higher concurrencies.\"]}]\n38:[\"$\",\"figure\",null,{\"className\":\"my-6\",\"style\":\"$undefined\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L2a\",null,{\"src\":\"/blog-assets/figures/2026-07-27-decode-context-parallelism/figure-5.png\",\"alt\":\"\",\"className\":\"rounded-lg max-w-full\",\"loading\":\"lazy\"}],\"\"]}]\n39:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"41-decode-context-parallelism-process\",\"className\":\"text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2 scroll-mt-24\",\"children\":\"4.1 Decode Context Parallelism Process\"}]\n3a:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"c"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"lassName\":\"my-4 leading-relaxed\",\"children\":[\"Standard Decode Context Parallelism keeps the communication pattern simple, following the rhythm \",[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"AllGather Q → Compute → AllGather + ReduceScatter\"}],\".\"]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"3b:[\"$\",\"ul\",null,{\"className\":\"my-4 ml-6 list-disc space-y-1.5\",\"children\":[\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"className\":\"leading-relaxed\",\"children\":[\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"my-4 leading-relaxed\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"AllGather Q:\"}],\" Each GPU has computed only a fragment of the query, but attention requires the full query vector to score against any key. An all-gather across the DCP group assembles a complete copy of the query on every GPU. This is cheap during decode because the query is a single token. As an opt-in alternative for MLA, \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/45964\",\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"className\":\"text-vllm-blue hover:underline\",\"children\":\"vLLM #45964\"}],\" can replicate the (small) query projection within each DCP group at load time so decode skips this query all-gather entirely (\",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"className\":\"px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded-md bg-muted font-mono text-sm break-all\",\"children\":\"VLLM_DCP_Q_REPLICATE=1\"}],\").\"]}],\"\\n\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"className\":\"leading-relaxed\",\"children\":[\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"my-4 leading-relaxed\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Compute:\"}],\" Each GPU runs attention between the gathered query and its \",[\"$\",\"em\",null,{\"children\":\"local\"}],\" slice of the KV cache. In vLLM this is \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"className\":\"px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded-md bg-muted font-mono text-sm break-all\",\"children\":\"k_up\"}],\" for MLA or \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"className\":\"px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded-md bg-muted font-mono text-sm break-all\",\"children\":\"tensor_broadcast\"}],\" for GQA.\"]}],\"\\n\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"className\":\"leading-relaxed\",\"children\":[\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"my-4 leading-relaxed\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":[\"AllGather + ReduceScatter (\",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"className\":\"px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded-md bg-muted font-mono text-sm break-all\",\"children\":\"cp_lse_ag_out_rs\"}],\"):\"]}],\" The partial results are combined into the true output. AllGather shares each GPU's partial output and LSE; the LSE values reweight and merge the partials (the online-softmax trick), and ReduceScatter sums them while handing each GPU back only its own head-slice.\"]}],\"\\n\"]}],\"\\n\"]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"3c:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"5-vllm-usage\",\"className\":\"text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3 scroll-mt-24\",\"children\":\"5. vLLM Usage\"}]\n3d:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"my-4 leading-relaxed\",\"children\":[\"DCP is enabled with a single extra argument, \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"className\":\"px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded-md bg-muted font-mono text-sm break-all\",\"children\":\"decode_context_parallel_size\"}],\", alongside your existing tensor-parallel setting.\"]}]\n3e:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"51-offline\",\"className\":\"text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2 scroll-mt-24\",\"children\":\"5.1 Offline\"}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"3f:[\"$\",\"figure\",null,{\"data-rehype-pretty-code-figure\":\"\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"relative group my-4 rounded-lg border bg-muted/50\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L57\",null,{}],[\"$\",\"pre\",null,{\"className\":\"overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm [\u0026\u003ecode]:block [\u0026\u003ecode]:w-fit [\u0026\u003ecode]:min-w-full font-mono\",\"style\":\"$undefined\",\"tabIndex\":\"0\",\"data-language\":\"python\",\"data-theme\":\"github-dark-default github-light-default\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"className\":\"$undefined\",\"data-language\":\"python\",\"data-theme\":\"github-dark-default github-light-default\",\"style\":{\"display\":\"grid\"},\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"data-line\":\"\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"style\":{\"--shiki-dark\":\"#FF7B72\",\"--shiki-light\":\"#CF222E\"},\"children\":\"from\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"style\":{\"--shiki-dark\":\"#E6EDF3\",\"--shiki-light\":\"#1F2328\"},\"children\":\" vllm \"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"style\":{\"--shiki-dark\":\"#FF7B72\",\"--shiki-light\":\"#CF222E\"},\"children\":\"import\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"style\":{\"--shiki-dark\":\"#79C0FF\",\"--shiki-light\":\"#0550AE\"},\"children\":\" LLM\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"style\":{\"--shiki-dark\":\"#E6EDF3\",\"--shiki-light\":\"#1F2328\"},\"children\":\", SamplingParams\"}]]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"data-line\":\"\",\"children\":\" \"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"data-line\":\"\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"style\":{\"--shiki-dark\":\"#E6EDF3\",\"--shiki-light\":\"#1F2328\"},\"children\":\"prompts \"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"style\":{\"--shiki-dark\":\"#FF7B72\",\"--shiki-light\":\"#CF222E\"},\"children\":\"=\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"style\